nto  black trappy territory  can you see a dazzling shot here  yup black has this amazing winning shot  knight captures e4 bam  so at this point now black is a clear  pawn up  and white cooperate very well let's say  if you take over knight then after  knight captures d4  castle and black castle indeed  with this extra pawn and some careful  play  black can easily score the full point  so nope that's not the way to play  and accordingly here the most critical  response is  knight captures c6 counter-attacking the  black queen  and then intending to take on e4  now if you run this position in any  chess engine the engine suggests that  you should play knight capture c3 and  even though the resulting position is  quite good for the black i think the  emerging end game  is not so clear cut as per the human  perspective  so instead of that i am going to  recommend this straightforward approach  bishop capture c3 okay  white response is force he has to take  this bishop  and after that yup you guessed it  d capture c6  now if you carefully look at this  position not only black is a clear pawn  up  but white has some shattered pawn  structure on the queen side  so if white avoid the queen trade let's  say queen to c2  then after bishop to f5 black get  a very good middle game with  healthy structure and upon advantage  so accordingly the only reply which  keeps white  in the game is queen to d4  attacking the two pieces  okay now black response is forced  he has to capture this queen and after  pawn captures d4  the dust is clear and one can easily see  that  black emerged with a clear pawn  advantage  and although white has a bishop pair i  think with the accurate play  this is great position for the black  camp  on you might think the trap is alright  well it's not the case because  still black can go for more with  bishop to e6 and  believe it or not but it completely  stopped  white to castle on the king's side  that is the most popular choice so let's  see  what is wrong after this natural move  now by force black is winning  the second pawn yup you heard it right  and the tricky sequence start with  knight to d6 attacking on c4  so white response is force he has to  play c5  and there comes knight to f5  pressurizing the d4 pawn  here it doesn't matter however white  defend on d4 with the bishop let's say  bishop to b2 is the most popular choice  after that we can simply castle  and as you can see very simple plan for  the black camp is  to double the rook on the d-file and  grab  the d-pawn  well you might think that's not possible  because  white can also double on d file but the  big problem is  in that process the a2 pawn hangs  let me demonstrate here first thing  first  if bishop to d3 this is a great mistake  here by white  as once again black has this tactical  resource  boom  so that after bishop captures d4 and the  move rook to d8  black get his piece back with the  interest  so that's not a good choice so the only  good move here  is rook to d1 but after that  rook to d8 white can see what is coming  up  so first he need to protect the a pawn  and it doesn't matter  whether the a3 or a4 let's say in one  game  a4 happened black indeed  double up the rook and white followed  the suit  with rook to d2 but what he missed is  after rook to d8 rook to d1  the winning shot for the black  yup bishop to b3 and  sadly white rook has to  go out of the d-file and after that  yup another pawn goes down the hill  with knight captures d4 and i'm sure  after this 19th move long trap  black should mop up the game very  quickly  that's it guys i hope you enjoy and  learn this  high profile trap in the mark cozy bind  remember if you open and continue with  this inaccurate move  bishop to e2 then don't forget to punish  him  with knight captures e4 and as  i have shown in this video all the  complication  leads to in the favor of the black  thank you for watching this video feel  free to like subscribe and comment  and i will meet y